.023468017578125, 0.034820556640625, -0.0254364013671875, -0.01074981689453125, 0.01042938232421875, -0.004802703857421875, 0.005062103271484375, -0.0103912353515625, -0.0155487060546875, 0.00914764404296875, 0.0108642578125, -0.00333404541015625, -0.0015935897827148438, -0.003902435302734375, 0.013671875, 0.0162811279296875, -0.0159149169921875, -0.001140594482421875, 0.00826263427734375, -0.01355743408203125, 0.017822265625, 0.0260162353515625, -0.0017843246459960938, 0.041412353515625, -0.00753021240234375, 0.03631591796875, 0.0038604736328125, 0.0003581047058105469, 0.0017747879028320312, 0.0137939453125, -0.0228118896484375, 0.01387786865234375, 0.018829345703125, -0.0199432373046875, 0.0235443115234375, -0.005191802978515625, -0.01520538330078125, 0.0245819091796875, 0.01157379150390625, 0.00879669189453125, -0.059783935546875, 0.0175628662109375, -0.0133209228515625, 0.0002703666687011719, 0.003620147705078125, 0.00782012939453125, 0.0059814453125, 0.0092315673828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Motivation**\n\nWe study how the order of synaptic inputs affects the firing behavior of sparse threshold-reset networks in the fast-synapse limit. This question is motivated by both biological and mathematical considerations: in real neural circuits, the precise timing and order of excitatory and inhibitory inputs can critically determine whether a neuron fires [1,2]. Mathematically, the fast-synapse limit ($\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$) is singular, and weak convergence of input measures does not always preserve the temporal order of events, potentially leading to discontinuous changes in network output. Understanding this sensitivity is essential for the rigorous analysis of large, sparse spiking networks and for interpreting the effects of synaptic delays and spike timing in computational neuroscience.\n\n**1.1. Notation and Weak Convergence of Measures**\n\nLet $\\mathcal{M}(\\mathbb{R})$ denote the space of finite signed Borel measures on $\\mathbb{R}$. Any $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{M}(\\mathbb{R})$ admits a Jordan decomposition $\\mu = \\mu^+ - \\mu^-$, where $\\mu^+, \\mu^-$ are finite nonnegative Borel measures. We say that a sequence $(\\mu_N)_{N\\geq 1}$ converges *weakly* to $\\mu$ if, for every bounded continuous function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$,\n\\[\n\\int f\\,d\\mu_N \\to \\int f\\,d\\mu \\quad \\text{as } N\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nFor signed measures, this is equivalent to requiring weak convergence of both $\\mu_N^+$ and $\\mu_N^-$ to $\\mu^+$ and $\\mu^-$, respectively. The term \u201ccomponentwise weak convergence\u201d refers to this property; see [5,6] for standard references.\n\n**1.2. Fast-Synapse Limit and Synaptic Kernels**\n\nLet $K_\\varepsilon \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$ be a family of nonnegative, compactly supported functions with $\\operatorname{supp}(K_\\varepsilon) \\subset [0,\\varepsilon]$ and $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. The *fast-synapse limit* is the regime $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$, in which all synaptic kernel delays vanish and $K_\\varepsilon$ converges weakly to the Dirac delta $\\delta_0$. However, weak convergence does not preserve the order of arrival of inputs, which can have dramatic effects on threshold-based dynamics.\n\n**1.3. Model Assumptions and Network Structure**\n\n- *Spike Processing Protocol*: Spikes are processed in strict temporal order. After a spike, the membrane potential is clamped at reset value for a fixed refractory period $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$.\n- *Synaptic Kernels*: All kernels $K_\\varepsilon$ are $C^\\infty$, nonnegative, compactly supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$, and satisfy $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. All synaptic delays are strictly positive.\n- *Dale Compatibility*: Each neuron is either purely excitatory or purely inhibitory.\n- *Typewise-Mixing*: Let $G_N$ be a random directed graph on $N$ vertices, partitioned into finitely many synaptic classes. For each class, the induced subgraph is such that, for any fixed neuron, the empirical distribution of incoming edges from that class converges in total variation to the class distribution as $N\\to\\infty$ (see [7] for formal definitions).\n\nThese assumptions ensure well-posedness of the dynamics, prevent pathological instantaneous feedback, and reflect biological constraints [1,2].\n\n**2. Model Specification**\n\nWe consider a leaky integrate-and-fire neuron with membrane potential $V(t)$ governed by\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = -\\lambda (V(t) - V_{\\mathrm{rest}}) + I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t),\n\\]\nwhere $\\lambda\\geq 0$ is the leak rate, $V_{\\mathrm{rest}}$ is the resting potential, and $I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t)$ is the total synaptic input. When $V(t)$ reaches threshold $\\theta$, the neuron emits a spike, $V$ is reset to $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$, and held at $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ for $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$.\n\n**2.1. Construction of Input Spike Trains**\n\nWe focus on the effect of two temporally close spikes, parameterized by a small delay $\\varepsilon>0$ and pulse masses $a>0$ (excitatory), $b>0$ (inhibitory):\n\n- *Excitatory-first protocol*: The neuron receives an excitatory spike at $t=0$ and an inhibitory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n- *Inhibitory-first protocol*: The neuron receives an inhibitory spike at $t=0$ and an excitatory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n\nEach spike is modeled as $a K_\\varepsilon(t)$ (excitatory) or $-b K_\\varepsilon(t-\\delta)$ (inhibitory, with $\\delta=0$ or $\\varepsilon$). If spikes overlap (i.e., $\\varepsilon$ is small), the total input is the sum of the corresponding kernels. For more than two spikes, the analysis extends by linearity, but we focus on the two-spike case for clarity.\n\n**2.2. Synaptic Input in the Network Case**\n\nFor a network, the synaptic input to neuron $i$ is\n\\[\nI_{\\mathrm{syn},i}(t) = \\sum_{j} w_{ij} \\sum_{k} K_\\varepsilon(t - t_{j,k} - d_{ij}),\n\\]\nwhere $w_{ij}$ is the synaptic weight from neuron $j$ to $i$, $t_{j,k}$ are the spike times of neuron $j$, and $d_{ij} > 0$ is the synaptic delay. The network is initialized identically in both protocols, differing only in the order of paired spikes as above.\n\n**3. Main Results: Order Sensitivity and Robustness**\n\nWe now state the main result for a single neuron, using the indicator function $\\mathbb{I}\\{\\cdot\\}$ to denote whether the neuron fires in a given protocol.\n\n**Theorem 1 (Order Sensitivity in the Fast-Synapse Limit).**  \nLet $K_\\varepsilon \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$ be nonnegative, supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$, and $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. Let $a, b > 0$, $\\varepsilon > 0$, and let $V(t)$ solve\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = -\\lambda (V(t) - V_{\\mathrm{rest}}) + a K_\\varepsilon(t) - b K_\\varepsilon(t-\\delta)\n\\]\nwith $V(0) = x$, threshold $\\theta$, and reset protocol as above. Consider two cases:\n- (i) $\\delta = \\varepsilon$ (excitation at $t=0$, inhibition at $t=\\varepsilon$),\n- (ii) $\\delta = 0$ (inhibition at $t=0$, excitation at $t=\\varepsilon$).\n\nAssume $\\varepsilon < \\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$, $\\lambda \\geq 0$, and $V_{\\mathrm{rest}}, V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ arbitrary. Then, in the limit $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$,\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0} \\mathbb{I}\\{\\text{neuron fires in protocol (i)}\\} = 1, \\quad \\lim_{\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0} \\mathbb{I}\\{\\text{neuron fires in protocol (ii)}\\} = 0\n\\]\nif and only if $x + a - b < \\theta \\leq x + a$.\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe analyze both cases as $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$:\n\n- *Protocol (i):* For $t \\in [0, \\varepsilon)$, $V(t)$ increases due to the excitatory input. Since $K_\\varepsilon$ is supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$ and integrates to $1$, the total excitatory increment is $a$. If $x + a \\geq \\theta$, $V(t)$ crosses threshold before $t = \\varepsilon$. After firing, $V$ is reset and clamped for $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}} > \\varepsilon$, so the subsequent inhibitory input has no effect. Thus, the neuron fires if and only if $x + a \\geq \\theta$.\n\n- *Protocol (ii):* The inhibitory input arrives at $t=0$, reducing $V$ by $b$ before any excitation. The subsequent excitatory input at $t=\\varepsilon$ increases $V$ by $a$, so the total is $x - b + a$. If $x - b + a < \\theta$, $V$ never reaches threshold.\n\nTherefore, the neuron fires in protocol (i) but not in protocol (ii) if and only if $x + a - b < \\theta \\leq x + a$. The limit as $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$ is justified by the smoothness and support of $K_\\varepsilon$, which ensures that the increments occur in the correct order and that the threshold crossing is"}}